hello-i386
----------

Intel XED reference checks
--------------------------

The external host tool in tests/xed is optional. It links an independently
installed, native Intel XED static kit; it does not link QEMU, and the QEMU
instruction plugin does not link XED. See https://github.com/intelxed/xed
for the kit build instructions (including the separate mbuild dependency).

From a configured QEMU build directory:

    make check-xed XED_KIT=/absolute/path/to/xed-kit

This checks instruction lengths, operand/address widths and unsigned
immediates against fixed reference cases. A missing kit is an error when
the target is explicitly invoked, not a normal build dependency.

To compare TCG's reported translation lengths with XED, build the test
plugins (make test-plugins), then run a controlled x86 guest with:

    -plugin tests/tcg/plugins/libinsn.so,xed-trace=on -d plugin -D xed-trace.log

Use the plugin's actual built filename on the host (e.g. libinsn.dylib on
some macOS configurations). The plugin records guest instruction bytes,
linear PC and length; it does not claim that each translation executed.

    make check-xed XED_KIT=/absolute/path/to/xed-kit \
        XED_TRACE=xed-trace.log XED_MODE=legacy32 XED_STACK=32 \
        XED_CHIP=pentium_mmx

Select real16, legacy16 or legacy32 and a stack width of 16 or 32.
The chip is pentium or pentium_mmx. Only compare a trace (or a deliberately
selected subset of records) from a known uniform code/stack mode; SeaBIOS
switches modes and the plugin does not infer them. XED failures, including
truncated instruction bytes and instructions that should fault on the
chosen chip, are candidates for investigation, not proof of a TCG bug.
This checks decoder metadata, not MMU accesses, segment limits or actual
instruction execution. No XED downloads are performed by the build.
